Tiger's <ctype.h> defines isdigit() as a macro:

    #define isdigit(c)  __istype((c), _CTYPE_D)

and boost::locale declares a *member function* of the same name. The
declaration expands into the macro body, which is not a declaration at all,
so GCC reports the collision as a cascade of nonsense:

    mo_lambda.cpp:257:14: error: expected identifier before '(' token
    mo_lambda.cpp:257:14: error: expected ',' or '...' before numeric constant
    mo_lambda.cpp:259:25: error: 'c' was not declared in this scope
    mo_lambda.cpp:275:29: error: invalid conversion from 'char' to 'int (*)(char)'

Modern macOS declares isdigit as a real function, so upstream never sees
this. Undefining the macro is enough -- the member function is self-contained
('0'<=c && c<='9') and nothing in this file wants the libc version.

--- libs/locale/src/shared/mo_lambda.cpp.orig	2021-04-16 00:00:00.000000000 -0400
+++ libs/locale/src/shared/mo_lambda.cpp	2021-04-16 00:00:00.000000000 -0400
@@ -8,6 +8,14 @@
 #include "mo_lambda.hpp"
 #include <string.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
+
+// Tiger's <ctype.h>, pulled in via the headers above, defines isdigit() as a
+// macro. This file declares a member function of the same name, which the
+// macro then eats. Drop the macro; the member function below does the job.
+#ifdef isdigit
+#undef isdigit
+#endif
+

 namespace boost {
 namespace locale {
